\title{The Anti-Fibonacci Sequence:\\ Quadratic Growth That Avoids the Golden Ratio}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-03}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The Fibonacci sequence $F(n+1) = F(n) + F(n-1)$ grows exponentially, and the ratio of its consecutive terms converges to the golden ratio $\varphi = (1+\sqrt5)/2$. We study a natural counterpoint arising from the greedy principle ``each new term dodges the running Fibonacci-style sum.'' This construction yields the \emph{anti-Fibonacci sequence} $A(0)=1$, $A(n+1) = A(n) + n$, whose terms are $1, 1, 2, 4, 7, 11, 16, 22, 29, 37, \dots$ We prove that this sequence is governed entirely by the closed-form identity $2A(n) + n = n^2 + 2$, equivalently $A(n) = 1 + \binom{n}{2}$. From this single relation we derive three principal results. First (density / quadratic growth), $A(n)/n^2 \to 1/2$, so the sequence is genuinely quadratic with leading coefficient $1/2$. Second (golden-ratio avoidance), the consecutive ratio $A(n+1)/A(n)$ converges to $1 \neq \varphi$; the sequence never approaches the golden ratio. Third (Fibonacci-coincidence characterization), the three-term relation $A(n+2) = A(n+1) + A(n)$ holds for exactly two indices, $n = 0$ and $n = 3$, and the sequence strictly undershoots the Fibonacci sum for all $n \ge 4$. We situate these results as the quadratic mirror image of the classical Fibonacci/golden-ratio theory and discuss a dichotomy for greedy ``avoid the combination'' sequences.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

The Fibonacci sequence is the archetype of iterated addition. Defined by $F(0)=0$, $F(1)=1$, and $F(n+1) = F(n) + F(n-1)$, it grows exponentially, and Binet's formula shows $F(n) \sim \varphi^n/\sqrt5$, where
\[
\varphi = \frac{1+\sqrt5}{2} \approx 1.618034
\]
is the golden ratio, the positive root of $x^2 = x + 1$. A defining feature is that the ratio of consecutive terms converges to $\varphi$: the golden ratio is an \emph{attractor} for a broad family of additive recurrences.

This paper investigates a deliberately contrarian construction. Instead of \emph{forming} Fibonacci-style sums, we ask each term to \emph{avoid} them: let each new term be the smallest positive value that keeps the sequence strictly increasing while sidestepping the Fibonacci sum of its predecessors. Carrying out this greedy ``dodge the sum'' recipe produces a sequence whose increments grow by exactly one at each step, and which therefore satisfies the clean additive recurrence
\begin{equation}
A(0) = 1, \qquad A(n+1) = A(n) + n. \tag{1}
\end{equation}
Its first terms are
\begin{equation}
1,\ 1,\ 2,\ 4,\ 7,\ 11,\ 16,\ 22,\ 29,\ 37,\ 46,\ 56,\ \dots \tag{2}
\end{equation}
These are the central polygonal (``lazy caterer'') numbers, shifted to include a repeated leading $1$: $A(n)$ is the maximum number of regions into which $n-1$ straight lines divide the plane.

We call $A$ the \emph{anti-Fibonacci sequence} because it is the structural opposite of the Fibonacci sequence. Where Fibonacci multiplies, $A$ accumulates; where Fibonacci grows exponentially, $A$ grows quadratically; where the Fibonacci ratio converges to $\varphi$, the anti-Fibonacci ratio converges to $1$; and where the Fibonacci relation always holds, for $A$ it holds only twice.

\subsection{Prior informal conjectures and their resolution}
The construction was first accompanied by three informal conjectures, which our analysis sharpens or corrects.
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{(a) Growth constant.} It was conjectured that $A(n) \sim n^2/4$. The closed form (Theorem~\ref{thm:closed}) shows the correct leading coefficient is $1/2$: $A(n)/n^2 \to 1/2$.
  \item \textbf{(b) Ratio behavior.} It was conjectured that $A(n+1)/A(n)$ oscillates in $[1,2]$ without converging. In fact (Theorem~\ref{thm:ratio}) it \emph{does} converge, monotonically after the second term, to $1$.
  \item \textbf{(c) Sum avoidance.} It was conjectured that $A$ always avoids being a Fibonacci sum. The precise statement (Theorem~\ref{thm:coin}) is that the three-term relation holds at exactly $n = 0$ and $n = 3$, with strict undershoot for every $n \ge 4$.
\end{itemize}

\section{The governing closed form}

\begin{definition}[Anti-Fibonacci sequence]
Define $A : \N \to \N$ by $A(0) = 1$ and $A(n+1) = A(n) + n$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Closed form]\label{thm:closed}
For all $n \in \N$,
\begin{equation}
2\,A(n) + n = n^2 + 2. \tag{3}
\end{equation}
Equivalently, over the reals,
\begin{equation}
A(n) = \frac{n^2 - n + 2}{2} = 1 + \frac{n(n-1)}{2} = 1 + \binom{n}{2}. \tag{4}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
We argue by induction on $n$. For $n = 0$: $2A(0) + 0 = 2 = 0^2 + 2$. Assume $2A(n) + n = n^2 + 2$. Then, using $A(n+1) = A(n) + n$,
\[
2A(n+1) + (n+1) = (2A(n) + n) + (2n + 1) = (n^2 + 2) + (2n + 1) = (n+1)^2 + 2,
\]
completing the induction and proving (3). Dividing (3) by $2$ and solving for $A(n)$ gives (4).
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Positivity]
$A(n) > 0$ for all $n$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
If $A(n) = 0$, then (3) gives $n = n^2 + 2$, i.e. $n^2 - n + 2 = 0$, which has no real solution (discriminant $1 - 8 < 0$).
\end{proof}

\section{Density: quadratic growth with leading coefficient $1/2$}

\begin{theorem}[Density / quadratic growth]\label{thm:density}
\[
\lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{A(n)}{n^2} = \frac{1}{2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By (4), for $n \ge 1$,
\[
\frac{A(n)}{n^2} = \frac{n^2 - n + 2}{2n^2} = \frac{1}{2}\left(1 - \frac{1}{n} + \frac{2}{n^2}\right).
\]
As $n \to \infty$, the bracket tends to $1$, so the expression tends to $1/2$.
\end{proof}

Thus $A(n) = \tfrac12 n^2 + O(n)$; this refutes the guess $A(n) \sim n^2/4$. Numerically, at $n = 10^6$ one finds $A(n)/n^2 = 0.4999995$ and $A(n) = 499{,}999{,}500{,}001$.

\begin{remark}
From (4), $A(n) = \tfrac{n^2}{2} - \tfrac{n}{2} + 1$ exactly, so $A(n) = \lfloor n^2/2 \rfloor + O(1)$ with an explicit, bounded error.
\end{remark}

\section{Golden-ratio avoidance}

\begin{theorem}[Ratio limit]\label{thm:ratio}
\[
\lim_{n \to \infty} \frac{A(n+1)}{A(n)} = 1.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $A(n+1) = A(n) + n$,
\[
\frac{A(n+1)}{A(n)} = 1 + \frac{n}{A(n)} = 1 + \frac{2n}{n^2 - n + 2} \longrightarrow 1,
\]
as the denominator grows quadratically while the numerator grows linearly. Equivalently, $A(n+1)/n^2 \to 1/2$ and $A(n)/n^2 \to 1/2$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:density}), and the quotient of these limits is $1$ since the common limit $1/2 \neq 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[The limit is not the golden ratio]\label{prop:notphi}
$1 \neq \varphi$, where $\varphi = (1+\sqrt5)/2$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Since $5 > 1$, $\sqrt5 > 1$, so $\varphi = (1+\sqrt5)/2 > (1+1)/2 = 1$. Hence $1 \neq \varphi$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Golden-ratio avoidance]
The consecutive ratio $A(n+1)/A(n)$ converges to $1$, and $1 \neq \varphi$. Consequently, unlike the Fibonacci sequence, whose consecutive ratios converge to $\varphi$, the anti-Fibonacci sequence never approaches the golden ratio; its ratio settles permanently at $1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorem~\ref{thm:ratio} and Proposition~\ref{prop:notphi}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Monotone approach, no oscillation]
The conjecture that the ratios oscillate in $[1,2]$ without converging is false. Explicitly $A(1)/A(0) = 1$, $A(2)/A(1) = 2$, and for $n \ge 2$ the ratio $1 + \frac{2n}{n^2-n+2}$ decreases monotonically to $1$: the map $t \mapsto \frac{2t}{t^2 - t + 2}$ has derivative with numerator $2(2 - t^2) < 0$ for $t \ge 2$. The observed ratios $1, 2, 2, 1.75, 1.571, 1.454, 1.375, \dots$ confirm the monotone decrease.
\end{remark}

\section{The Fibonacci-coincidence set}

\begin{theorem}[Fibonacci coincidences]\label{thm:coin}
For all $n \in \N$,
\[
A(n+2) = A(n+1) + A(n) \iff n = 0 \ \text{or}\ n = 3.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By definition $A(n+2) = A(n+1) + (n+1)$. Cancelling $A(n+1)$, the relation is equivalent to $A(n) = n + 1$. By (4),
\[
1 + \frac{n(n-1)}{2} = n + 1 \iff n(n-1) = 2n \iff n^2 = 3n \iff n(n-3) = 0,
\]
which over $\N$ holds iff $n = 0$ or $n = 3$.
\end{proof}

The two coincidences are $A(2) = 2 = 1 + 1 = A(1) + A(0)$ and $A(5) = 11 = 7 + 4 = A(4) + A(3)$.

\begin{theorem}[Eventual strict undershoot]
For every $n \ge 4$, $\ A(n+2) < A(n+1) + A(n)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The inequality is equivalent to $n + 1 < A(n) = 1 + \tfrac{n(n-1)}{2}$, i.e. $n < \tfrac{n(n-1)}{2}$, i.e. $2 < n - 1$, i.e. $n > 3$.
\end{proof}

\section{The Fibonacci / anti-Fibonacci duality}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\toprule
Feature & Fibonacci $F$ & Anti-Fibonacci $A$ \\
\midrule
Recurrence & $F(n+1) = F(n) + F(n-1)$ & $A(n+1) = A(n) + n$ \\
Closed form & $F(n) = (\varphi^n - \psi^n)/\sqrt5$ & $A(n) = 1 + \binom{n}{2}$ \\
Growth & exponential, $\sim \varphi^n/\sqrt5$ & quadratic, $\sim n^2/2$ \\
Increment & grows exponentially & grows by $1$ (equals $n$) \\
Ratio limit & $\varphi = 1.618\dots$ & $1$ \\
Fibonacci relation & for all $n$ & only at $n \in \{0,3\}$ \\
Governing constant & $\varphi$ (root of $x^2=x+1$) & $1$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Where Fibonacci's DNA is the quadratic irrational $\varphi$, the anti-Fibonacci sequence's DNA is the integer $1$. The two are the exponential and quadratic prototypes of iterated addition.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithm}[Recurrence generation]
Generate $A(0), \dots, A(N)$ by iterating $A(k+1) = A(k) + k$. Time $O(N)$, space $O(N)$ (or $O(1)$ for the last term only).
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Closed-form evaluation]
Compute $A(n) = 1 + n(n-1)/2$ in $O(1)$ integer arithmetic, enabling instant evaluation at astronomically large $n$ and an independent cross-check against Algorithm~7.1.
\end{algorithm}

\begin{algorithm}[Coincidence and undershoot scan]
For a range of indices, test $A(n+2) = A(n+1) + A(n)$; return the coincidence set (provably $\{0,3\}$) and verify strict undershoot for $n \ge 4$.
\end{algorithm}

\section{Applications and interpretation}

The anti-Fibonacci sequence is a laboratory example of how a small change to a generation rule flips a system between growth regimes. The Fibonacci rule couples each term to two predecessors, manufacturing exponential growth and an irrational ratio limit. The anti-Fibonacci rule degrades this coupling to a fixed increment, so exponential growth collapses to quadratic and the ratio limit collapses from $\varphi$ to $1$: the sequence is exactly the boundary case between exponential and polynomial regimes. The closed form $A(n) = 1 + \binom{n}{2}$ also ties the sequence to combinatorial geometry (regions cut by lines) and to triangular numbers.

\section{Discussion and future work}

A single closed-form invariant, $2A(n) + n = n^2 + 2$, reduces growth rate, ratio limit, and the exact coincidence set to elementary algebra. We highlight three directions.

\paragraph{A ratio-limit dichotomy.} For the greedy family in which each new term is the smallest positive integer avoiding $p\cdot(\text{previous}) + q\cdot(\text{second previous})$, we conjecture polynomial growth with ratio limit $1$ exactly when the avoided combination is degenerate (single-term dominated), and otherwise exponential growth with ratio tending to the dominant root of $x^2 = px + q$. The additive rule is the boundary case.

\paragraph{Finiteness of coincidence sets.} For $A(n+1) = A(n) + f(n)$ with $f$ polynomial, we conjecture the Fibonacci-coincidence set is finite of size at most $\deg(f)+1$, being the integer-root set of one explicit polynomial, since the relation reduces to $A(n) = f(n+1)$.

\paragraph{Sharp crossover with Fibonacci.} We conjecture the anti-Fibonacci numbers exceed the Fibonacci numbers for only finitely many indices, with an explicit crossover index; $\varphi^2 = \varphi + 1$ forces $F(n) \sim \varphi^n$, which any polynomial eventually loses to.

\section{Conclusion}

The anti-Fibonacci sequence $A(0)=1$, $A(n+1) = A(n) + n$ is the quadratic mirror of the Fibonacci sequence. Governed by $2A(n) + n = n^2 + 2$, it grows quadratically with leading coefficient $1/2$; its consecutive ratios converge monotonically to $1 \neq \varphi$; and it satisfies the three-term Fibonacci relation at exactly $n = 0$ and $n = 3$, undershooting it for all larger $n$. Where iterated addition of two terms breeds the golden ratio, iterated addition of a growing increment breeds none: the anti-Fibonacci sequence climbs forever while its ratios come quietly to rest at $1$.

